@ 6.1.1 - Proto-Omotic to North Omotic
# citation: Mecislau, from Ehret, Christopher (1995), Reconstructing Proto-Afroasiatic (Proto-Afrasian): Vowels, Tone, Consonants, and Vocabulary (Voices from Asia)
	{ u, o } > { i, e }
	e > i / #N_C
	e > i / :{ #l_P, #l_C:[+voice] }:
	e > i / #b_
	e > i / p_r
	e > i / :{ #{s,ʃ,t͡sʼ}_kʷ, #{s,ʃ,t͡sʼ}_ʔ }:
	{ e, o } > { i, u } / #C_P
	{ e, o } > { i, u } / #(ʔ)_C
	{ e, o } > { i, u } / :{ #{kʼ,x}_tʼ, #{kʼ,x}_t͡sʼ }:
	{ e, o } > { i, u } / #(ʔ)_C$
	{ e, o } > { i, u } / :{ #P_t͡sʼ, #P_t͡ʃʼ }:
	a > o / #{z,d͡ʒ}_P
	{ e, e:[+long] } > { i, i:[+long] } / :{ #C:[+strident]_d, #C:[+strident]_n, #C:[+strident]_r }:
	Cw > C
	V:[+long] > V / #[+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo, -voice]_C
	u > u:[+long] / #S:[+voice]_P:[-voice]
	V:[+long] > V / #C_C$
	N > ∅ / :{ V_C:[+strident], V_p }: